SPU GAS: keep the nop opcode distinct from the generic .nop directive.

SPU accepts pseudo-ops without a leading dot. The generic nop directive
therefore intercepts GCC's "nop 127", interpreting the register operand
as a byte count and emitting 128 bytes rather than one 4-byte instruction.
This inflates code, invalidates GCC's layout estimates and can overflow
branch-hint displacements.

Use the same target-local dispatch rule as set and eqv: bare nop is an
instruction, while explicit .nop retains the generic directive behavior.

--- a/gas/config/tc-spu.c
+++ b/gas/config/tc-spu.c
@@ -96,5 +96,9 @@ const pseudo_typeS md_pseudo_table[] =
   {"string", stringer, 8 + 1},
   {"word", spu_cons, 4},
+  /* A nop operand is a register, not the generic directive's byte count.
+     Keep the directive available under its explicit dotted spelling.  */
+  {"nop", NULL, 0},
+  {".nop", s_nop, 0},
   /* Force set to be treated as an instruction.  */
   {"set", NULL, 0},
   {".set", s_set, 0},
